/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/az-802.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("az-802", [["ws1",0,1,"The DC holding the RID master role suffered a motherboard failure and will be scrapped. New users can no longer be created once the other DCs run out of their RID pools. What should you do?",["Transfer the role normally from Active Directory Users and Computers","Seize the role on a healthy DC with -Force","Restore the failed DC from backup onto new hardware","Promote a new DC; the role moves automatically"],1,"When the holder is permanently gone you seize the role with -Force and then clean up the old DC's metadata. A normal transfer needs the current holder online, which is why it fails here.","Deploy and manage AD DS",["A transfer is a graceful handoff that needs the current role holder online to agree; the failed DC cannot respond, so the transfer fails.",null,"Restoring a DC that held a role and is being retired risks it coming back with stale state; the supported path is to seize the role and clean up the dead DC's metadata.","FSMO roles never move on their own when a new DC is promoted; they stay assigned to the dead DC until you transfer or seize them."]],["ws2",0,1,"Which FSMO role is the authority for time synchronization in a domain and the place urgent password changes are replicated to first?",["Infrastructure master","RID master","PDC emulator","Domain naming master"],2,"The PDC emulator is the domain time source and receives urgent password changes first. The infrastructure master only updates cross-domain object references.","Deploy and manage AD DS",["The infrastructure master only updates references to objects in other domains; it has nothing to do with time or password changes.","The RID master hands out pools of relative IDs for creating security principals; it is not the time source or the password-change target.",null,"The domain naming master is a forest-wide role that controls adding and removing domains; it plays no part in time sync or urgent password replication."]],["ws3",0,1,"A branch office has a slow WAN link and no secure room. Staff need local sign-in, but head office does not want passwords stored on the branch DC. What should you deploy?",["A read-only domain controller with a password replication policy","A writable DC protected with BitLocker and a locked cabinet","A global catalog server in the branch","A DNS secondary zone only"],0,"An RODC stores only the passwords you allow in its password replication policy and cannot originate changes. A writable DC with BitLocker still holds every password hash.","Deploy and manage AD DS",[null,"BitLocker protects the disk at rest, but a writable DC still stores every password hash and can be abused if the server is stolen or tampered with while running.","A global catalog is a full writable DC with extra forest-wide data, so it still stores all passwords and does nothing to limit what is cached.","A secondary DNS zone only answers name queries; it cannot authenticate users, so staff would still sign in across the slow WAN."]],["ws4",0,1,"You are adding a DC to a remote site whose WAN link is too slow for the initial directory copy. What reduces the traffic during promotion?",["Enable universal group membership caching","Create a one-way trust to the site","Raise the domain functional level","Install from media created with ntdsutil IFM"],3,"Install From Media seeds the new DC from a local copy of the database, so only changes since the IFM snapshot replicate over the WAN. Universal group caching affects logons, not promotion.","Deploy and manage AD DS",["Universal group membership caching reduces global catalog lookups at logon in sites without a GC; it does not shrink the initial database replication when promoting a DC.","Trusts connect domains or forests, not sites, and a trust does nothing to reduce the amount of data a new DC replicates.","Raising the domain functional level unlocks features; it has no effect on how much data replicates during DC promotion.",null]],["ws5",0,1,"Users in a new branch sign in slowly because they authenticate against DCs in head office even though a local DC exists. What is the most likely cause?",["The branch DC is not a global catalog","The DNS zone is not AD-integrated","The branch IP subnet is not associated with the branch site","The site link cost is too low"],2,"Clients find their site from the subnet objects. If the branch subnet is missing, clients are treated as site-less and may pick any DC. The GC setting affects universal group lookups, not DC selection.","Deploy and manage AD DS",["A non-GC DC can still authenticate users; missing GC status affects universal group lookups at logon, not which DC a client picks.","AD-integrated zones change how DNS data is stored and replicated, but SRV records for site DCs still work in a standard zone; it does not cause clients to use the wrong site.",null,"Site link cost affects replication and next-closest-site selection; a low cost does not make clients inside a correctly mapped site bypass their local DC."]],["ws6",0,1,"Which command gives a quick summary of replication failures and largest deltas for every DC in the forest?",["repadmin /replsummary","dcdiag /test:replications /v","nltest /dsgetdc","gpresult /r"],0,"repadmin /replsummary lists failures and largest deltas per source and destination DC in one table. dcdiag /test:replications checks the DC it runs against and gives verbose per-DC output, not a forest-wide summary.","Deploy and manage AD DS",[null,"dcdiag /test:replications checks replication on the DC it runs against with verbose output, rather than giving one forest-wide summary table.","nltest /dsgetdc locates a DC for a domain; it reports nothing about replication failures or deltas.","gpresult /r shows the Resultant Set of Policy for a user and computer; it has nothing to do with AD replication."]],["ws7",0,1,"After an acquisition, users in contoso.com need to reach resources in every domain of the fabrikam.com forest, and fabrikam users need the same in reverse. Both forests run at Windows Server 2016 level. Which trust should you create?",["An external trust between the two root domains","A shortcut trust between the two forest roots","A realm trust","A two-way forest trust"],3,"A forest trust is transitive across all domains of both forests. An external trust is non-transitive and only links the two named domains.","Deploy and manage AD DS",["An external trust is non-transitive and links only the two named domains, so users would not reach every domain in the other forest.","A shortcut trust optimizes authentication paths between domains inside the same forest; it cannot join two separate forests.","A realm trust connects AD to a non-Windows Kerberos v5 realm, not to another AD forest.",null]],["ws8",0,1,"After creating a forest trust, you want partner users to reach only two file servers in your forest instead of everything. What should you configure?",["Selective authentication with Allowed to Authenticate on the two servers","SID filtering quarantine","A one-way outgoing external trust to each partner domain","Universal group membership caching on the partner-facing site"],0,"Selective authentication blocks access by default and lets you grant Allowed to Authenticate per computer object. SID filtering removes foreign SIDs but does not limit which servers users can reach.","Deploy and manage AD DS",[null,"SID filtering strips SIDs from other domains to block SID-history elevation; it does not restrict which servers trusted users can authenticate to.","Replacing the forest trust with external trusts changes transitivity, but users of the trusted domain could still reach every server in the trusting domain.","Universal group membership caching speeds up logons in sites without a GC; it has no effect on what partner users can access."]],["ws9",0,1,"You try to create the first group managed service account and receive an error that the key distribution service has no root key. What should you run first?",["Install-ADServiceAccount","Add-KdsRootKey","New-ADFineGrainedPasswordPolicy","Set-ADAccountControl"],1,"A gMSA needs a KDS root key to derive passwords. Install-ADServiceAccount is run later on each host that will use the account.","Deploy and manage AD DS",["Install-ADServiceAccount is run later on each host that will use the gMSA; it cannot work until a KDS root key exists and the account is created.",null,"New-ADFineGrainedPasswordPolicy creates a PSO for user password rules; gMSA passwords come from the KDS root key, not a PSO.","Set-ADAccountControl changes account flags such as delegation settings; it does not create the KDS root key the gMSA needs."]],["ws10",0,1,"Admins need 15-character passwords while everyone else keeps the 8-character domain policy. What is the supported way to do this?",["Link a password GPO to the Admins OU","Edit the Default Domain Controllers Policy","Create a PSO and apply it to a global security group of admins","Set the minimum password length on each admin account's properties in ADUC"],2,"Fine-grained password policies (PSOs) apply to users or global security groups. A password policy GPO linked to an OU does not affect domain accounts.","Deploy and manage AD DS",["Password settings in a GPO linked to an OU apply only to local accounts on computers in that OU, not to domain user accounts.","The Default Domain Controllers Policy is not where domain account password policy is read from, and editing it would not target only admins anyway.",null,"ADUC has no per-account minimum password length setting; per-user or per-group password rules require a PSO."]],["ws11",0,1,"An admin accidentally deleted an OU containing 200 users. The AD Recycle Bin was enabled last year. What restores the users with their group memberships and attributes?",["An authoritative restore from last night's system state backup","Recreating the users from a CSV file","A non-authoritative restore on one DC","Restore-ADObject or the Deleted Objects container in ADAC"],3,"With the Recycle Bin enabled, deleted objects keep all attributes and can be restored online. An authoritative restore also works but needs a DC offline in DSRM and loses changes made since the backup.","Deploy and manage AD DS",["An authoritative restore does work but requires taking a DC offline into DSRM and loses any changes made since the backup, so it is not the right tool when the Recycle Bin is on.","Recreating accounts from CSV produces new objects with new SIDs, losing group memberships, ACL access and other attributes.","A non-authoritative restore would simply be overwritten when the DC receives the deletion from its replication partners.",null]],["ws12",0,1,"You want the help desk to reset passwords only for users in the Sales OU without adding them to any admin group. What should you use?",["Add them to Account Operators","The Delegation of Control Wizard on the Sales OU","A GPO with User Rights Assignment","A fine-grained password policy"],1,"Delegation of Control grants the specific permission on that OU only. Account Operators applies domain-wide and is far broader.","Deploy and manage AD DS",["Account Operators is a built-in group with broad rights across the whole domain, far more than resetting passwords in one OU.",null,"User Rights Assignment controls privileges like logging on locally or shutting down a system; it cannot grant password-reset rights on AD objects in one OU.","A fine-grained password policy sets password length and lockout rules; it does not give anyone permission to reset passwords."]],["ws13",0,1,"Your company wants cloud sign-in to keep working if the on-premises data center is offline and wants leaked-credential reports in Entra ID Protection. Which sign-in method should you pick in Entra Connect?",["Password hash synchronization","Pass-through authentication","Federation with AD FS","Seamless SSO only"],0,"PHS lets Entra ID validate passwords itself and enables leaked-credential detection. PTA depends on on-premises agents and DCs being reachable.","Deploy and manage AD DS",[null,"Pass-through authentication validates passwords through on-premises agents and DCs, so cloud sign-in stops when the data center is down, and leaked-credential detection needs PHS.","AD FS federation relies on on-premises federation servers, so sign-in fails if the data center is offline.","Seamless SSO only silently signs in domain-joined devices on the corporate network; it is not a sign-in method on its own and must pair with PHS or PTA."]],["ws14",0,1,"Compliance says passwords must be validated by on-premises DCs at sign-in, but the company does not want to run AD FS. What should you configure?",["Password hash sync with a second Entra Connect server in staging mode","Entra Domain Services","Password writeback","Pass-through authentication with at least three agents"],3,"PTA validates passwords against on-premises AD through agents; running several agents gives high availability. PHS validates in the cloud, which breaks the requirement.","Deploy and manage AD DS",["Password hash sync validates passwords in Entra ID, not on-premises, and a staging server only adds failover of the sync engine.","Entra Domain Services provides managed domain services in Azure; it does not make cloud sign-ins validate against on-premises DCs.","Password writeback sends cloud password changes back to AD; it does not change where sign-in validation happens.",null]],["ws15",0,1,"You need a second Entra Connect Sync server ready to take over if the primary fails, without it exporting changes. What should you configure?",["A second active server with the same scope","Password writeback on both servers","Staging mode on the second server","Cloud Sync in parallel on the same OU"],2,"A staging mode server imports and syncs but does not export, so it can be switched to active if the primary fails. Two active Connect Sync servers are not supported.","Deploy and manage AD DS",["Two active Entra Connect Sync servers exporting to the same tenant is unsupported and causes conflicts; only one server can export.","Password writeback is a feature that sends password changes to AD; it does not create a standby sync server.",null,"Running Cloud Sync and Connect Sync over the same objects is not a supported failover design and both would export changes."]],["ws16",0,1,"A GPO linked at the domain sets a desktop wallpaper. The Finance OU has Block Inheritance enabled, yet Finance users still get the wallpaper. Why?",["Loopback processing is on","The GPO has a WMI filter","The domain link is Enforced","The GPO is linked at the site"],2,"An Enforced link overrides Block Inheritance. A WMI filter can only stop a GPO from applying, not force it past a block.","Deploy and manage AD DS",["Loopback processing changes which user settings apply based on the computer's location; it does not override Block Inheritance on an OU.","A WMI filter can only prevent a GPO from applying; it cannot push a GPO past Block Inheritance.",null,"A GPO linked at the site would also be stopped by Block Inheritance unless that link were Enforced, so the site link alone does not explain it."]],["ws17",0,1,"On Remote Desktop Session Host servers you want user settings from a GPO linked to the servers' OU to apply to anyone who signs in, regardless of where the user account lives. What should you enable?",["Loopback processing in Replace or Merge mode","Block Inheritance on the users' OU","Security filtering that grants Apply to Authenticated Users on the user OU","The Central Store"],0,"Loopback processing applies user settings based on the computer's location. Block Inheritance only affects which GPOs reach an OU.","Deploy and manage AD DS",[null,"Block Inheritance stops parent GPOs from reaching the users' OU; it would not make the RDS servers' GPO apply user settings.","User settings still come from GPOs in the user's own OU path; security filtering alone cannot make a GPO linked to the servers' OU apply to users elsewhere.","The Central Store holds ADMX templates in SYSVOL; it has no effect on which user settings apply."]],["ws18",0,1,"A GPO should apply only to members of the Kiosk group within an OU. What should you change?",["Add a WMI filter for the group name","Change security filtering from Authenticated Users to the Kiosk group","Link the GPO to the Kiosk group","Set the GPO status to User configuration settings disabled"],1,"Security filtering controls who gets Apply Group Policy; since 2016 the computers also need Read permission. GPOs cannot be linked to groups, only to sites, domains and OUs.","Deploy and manage AD DS",["WMI filters query system properties like OS version, not group membership, and are the wrong tool for this.",null,"GPOs can only be linked to sites, domains and OUs; they cannot be linked to a group.","Disabling user configuration settings affects every target of the GPO; it does not limit the GPO to the Kiosk group."]],["ws19",0,1,"You are consolidating a child domain into another forest with ADMT. Users must keep reaching file servers in the old domain until their ACLs are updated. What should you migrate with the accounts?",["Their password hashes only","SID history","Their roaming profiles only","The old domain's GPO links"],1,"SID history puts the old SID on the new account so resource ACLs that still reference it keep working. Passwords can be migrated too, but they do not grant access to old ACLs.","Deploy and manage AD DS",["Migrating passwords lets users sign in with the same password but does not give the new account access to resources whose ACLs reference the old SID.",null,"Roaming profiles hold user settings and files; migrating them does not grant access to old file server ACLs.","GPO links are not migrated with user accounts, and GPOs do not grant file server access in the source domain."]],["ws20",0,2,"Ten admins need browser access to manage 200 servers through Windows Admin Center from their own laptops. How should you install it?",["Desktop mode on each admin's laptop","Gateway mode on a Windows Server with a trusted certificate","On every managed server","As an Azure VM extension installed on each of the 200 on-premises servers"],1,"Gateway mode serves many admins through a browser from one server. Desktop mode is single-user on a client.","Manage Windows Server instances and workloads in a hybrid environment",["Desktop mode is a single-user installation on one machine, so each admin would need their own copy and there is no shared management point.",null,"Windows Admin Center is not installed on each managed server; the gateway connects to them remotely over WinRM.","The Windows Admin Center Azure VM extension is for Azure VMs, and installing it per server would not give 10 admins a single shared gateway for on-premises servers."]],["ws21",0,2,"Admins using a Windows Admin Center gateway get prompted for credentials again when a tool connects on to a second server. What removes the double hop without CredSSP?",["Resource-based constrained delegation that trusts the gateway","Enable WinRM over HTTP","Add admins to Remote Desktop Users on every target server","Disable UAC remote restrictions"],0,"Resource-based constrained delegation on the target lets the gateway pass Kerberos credentials on. WinRM over HTTP does nothing about delegation.","Manage Windows Server instances and workloads in a hybrid environment",[null,"Using WinRM over HTTP changes only the transport and encryption of the session; it does not allow the gateway to delegate credentials to a second server.","Remote Desktop Users membership grants RDP sign-in, which has nothing to do with Kerberos delegation from the gateway.","UAC remote restrictions affect local accounts' admin tokens over the network; disabling them does not solve the Kerberos double hop."]],["ws22",0,2,"Help desk staff must restart the print spooler on file servers but must not be local admins. Which solution fits?",["Add them to Remote Management Users only","Give them RDP access with a GPO that hides the Services console","A JEA endpoint whose role capability allows only Restart-Service -Name Spooler","Share the local admin password through Windows LAPS with auto-rotation after use"],2,"JEA exposes only the listed cmdlets and parameters, running as a virtual account. Remote Management Users alone gives a normal PowerShell session with the user's own limited rights, which cannot restart the service.","Manage Windows Server instances and workloads in a hybrid environment",["Remote Management Users only allows connecting to PowerShell remoting with the user's own rights, which are not enough to restart a service.","Hiding the Services console in a GPO does not stop other tools, and RDP access with a hidden console still would not give the right to restart the service.",null,"Sharing the local admin password, even rotated by LAPS, gives help desk full admin rights, which breaks the requirement."]],["ws23",0,2,"You want to apply Azure Policy, tags and Azure RBAC to 300 on-premises Windows Servers without migrating them. What must you do first?",["Install the Azure Monitor agent","Deploy Windows Admin Center gateway","Create a site-to-site VPN","Onboard them to Azure Arc with the Connected Machine agent"],3,"Arc projects each server as an Azure resource so Policy, tags and RBAC can target it. The Monitor agent on its own does not create that resource.","Manage Windows Server instances and workloads in a hybrid environment",["The Azure Monitor agent collects logs and metrics but does not create an Azure resource representation for Policy, tags or RBAC to target.","Windows Admin Center manages servers but does not project them into Azure Resource Manager.","A site-to-site VPN provides network connectivity only; servers still do not appear as Azure resources.",null]],["ws24",0,2,"You must onboard 300 servers to Azure Arc through a scheduled script without anyone signing in interactively. What do you need?",["A service principal with the Azure Connected Machine Onboarding role","A Global Administrator account in the script","A user account with Owner on the subscription and MFA turned off for the script","An Azure Bastion host"],0,"The at-scale onboarding script authenticates with a service principal limited to onboarding. Putting a Global Admin or Owner credential in a script breaks least privilege.","Manage Windows Server instances and workloads in a hybrid environment",[null,"A Global Administrator is an Entra directory role with far more power than onboarding needs, and embedding its credentials in a script breaks least privilege.","Owner is far too broad for onboarding, and turning off MFA for a user account to run a script is a serious security weakness.","Azure Bastion provides browser-based RDP/SSH to Azure VMs; it has nothing to do with onboarding on-premises servers to Arc."]],["ws25",0,2,"You want to report which Arc-enabled and Azure Windows Servers do not meet a security baseline inside the OS, without changing anything yet. What should you assign?",["An Azure RBAC deny assignment","A machine configuration (guest configuration) policy in audit mode","An NSG on the servers' subnet","A Defender for Cloud workflow automation that triggers on new recommendations"],1,"Machine configuration policies read settings inside the guest OS and can audit before you choose to enforce. RBAC deny assignments control Azure resource access, not OS settings.","Manage Windows Server instances and workloads in a hybrid environment",["RBAC deny assignments block actions on Azure resources; they cannot read or report on settings inside the guest OS.",null,"An NSG filters network traffic to the subnet; it cannot evaluate OS configuration against a baseline.","Workflow automation reacts to Defender for Cloud alerts or recommendations by triggering Logic Apps; it does not audit guest OS settings against a baseline itself."]],["ws26",0,2,"You need to patch Azure VMs and Arc-enabled on-premises servers every second Tuesday in a set window from one place. What should you use?",["Windows Server Update Services on each site with a downstream replica server in Azure","A Custom Script Extension that runs wuauclt","Azure Update Manager with a maintenance configuration","Azure Backup policies"],2,"Update Manager assesses and installs updates on Azure and Arc machines and schedules them with maintenance configurations. WSUS is on-premises only and not centrally scheduled from Azure.","Manage Windows Server instances and workloads in a hybrid environment",["WSUS is an on-premises update server with no scheduling from Azure, and a replica in Azure still would not orchestrate Azure and Arc machines from one place with maintenance windows.","A Custom Script Extension can run a one-off command, but wuauclt is deprecated and this gives no assessment, reporting or maintenance-window scheduling.",null,"Azure Backup protects data with backup policies; it does not install updates."]],["ws27",0,2,"An Azure Automation runbook must restart a service on an on-premises server that has no inbound connectivity from Azure. What should you set up?",["A hybrid runbook worker on a server in that network","A public IP on the server","An Azure Function with a VNet trigger","A point-to-site VPN for the runbook"],0,"A hybrid runbook worker polls Azure Automation outbound and runs jobs locally. A public IP would expose the server and still would not give the runbook a way in.","Manage Windows Server instances and workloads in a hybrid environment",[null,"A public IP would expose the server to the internet and still would not give the runbook a way to execute commands on it.","Azure Functions do not have a VNet trigger, and they are not the component that runs Automation runbooks on-premises.","A point-to-site VPN connects individual client computers to an Azure VNet; a runbook cannot use it to reach an on-premises server."]],["ws28",0,2,"You must replace an old Windows Server 2012 R2 file server with a new server, copy its shares and permissions, and have clients keep using the old server name without remapping drives. What is designed for this?",["Robocopy with /MIR and a DNS CNAME","Storage Migration Service with cutover","DFS Replication between the servers, followed by a namespace switch","Azure Migrate server migration"],1,"Storage Migration Service inventories, transfers and then cuts over the source name and IP to the destination. Robocopy moves files but not share configuration or identity, and a CNAME needs extra SPN and SMB alias work.","Manage Windows Server instances and workloads in a hybrid environment",["Robocopy copies files and NTFS ACLs but not share definitions, and a DNS CNAME needs extra SPN and SMB alias configuration to work.",null,"DFS Replication plus a namespace switch would require clients to use the new namespace path, so drives mapped to the old server name would break.","Azure Migrate moves whole servers to Azure VMs; it is not designed to transfer shares to a new on-premises server and take over the old name."]],["ws29",0,2,"You need to discover 150 on-premises Hyper-V VMs, get Azure sizing and cost estimates, and then replicate them to Azure. Which service fits?",["Azure Migrate","Azure Site Recovery alone","Storage Migration Service","Azure File Sync"],0,"Azure Migrate covers discovery, assessment and server migration to Azure. Site Recovery replicates for DR but provides no assessment or sizing.","Manage Windows Server instances and workloads in a hybrid environment",[null,"Site Recovery can replicate VMs to Azure but has no discovery, sizing or cost assessment.","Storage Migration Service migrates file servers' data and shares; it does not assess or migrate whole VMs to Azure.","Azure File Sync syncs file shares with Azure Files; it does not discover or migrate VMs."]],["ws30",0,3,"You need a Hyper-V VM with Secure Boot and a virtual TPM so the guest can use BitLocker. Which VM type do you create?",["Generation 1 with a legacy network adapter","Generation 1 with an IDE boot disk","Generation 2","Any generation with dynamic memory"],2,"Secure Boot and vTPM require a Gen 2 (UEFI) VM. Gen 1 uses BIOS and has no vTPM.","Manage virtual machines",["Generation 1 VMs use BIOS firmware and support neither Secure Boot nor a virtual TPM, regardless of the network adapter.","Generation 1 VMs boot from IDE with BIOS firmware and cannot have Secure Boot or a vTPM.",null,"Dynamic memory is unrelated to firmware; Secure Boot and vTPM require a Generation 2 VM."]],["ws31",0,3,"You enable nested virtualization but Hyper-V will not install inside the VM. Which setting is most likely still wrong?",["Dynamic memory is still enabled on the VM","The VM uses a VHDX disk","The VM is generation 2","Guest services integration component is disabled on the VM"],0,"Nested virtualization needs static memory and ExposeVirtualizationExtensions set while the VM is off. VHDX and Gen 2 are fine.","Manage virtual machines",[null,"VHDX is the normal disk format for Hyper-V VMs and does not block nested virtualization.","Generation 2 VMs fully support nested virtualization, so the generation is not the problem.","Guest services is an integration component for copying files into the VM; it has no bearing on exposing virtualization extensions."]],["ws32",0,3,"You need 20 lab VMs from one sysprepped base image while saving disk space. What should each VM use?",["A fixed-size VHDX copy of the base image for each of the 20 VMs","A pass-through physical disk","A shared VHDX","A differencing disk whose parent is the base VHDX"],3,"Differencing disks store only changes against a read-only parent. Fixed copies would use the full size 20 times.","Manage virtual machines",["Fixed-size copies allocate the full disk size for every VM, using the most space possible instead of saving it.","A pass-through disk dedicates an entire physical disk to one VM; it cannot be shared as a base for 20 VMs.","A shared VHDX is for guest clusters that need shared storage, not for many independent VMs based on one image.",null]],["ws33",0,3,"Before patching a domain-joined application VM you want a restore point that uses VSS inside the guest so data is application-consistent. Which option fits?",["A production checkpoint","A standard checkpoint","Exporting the VM while running with saved state","Pausing the VM"],0,"Production checkpoints use VSS in the guest for a consistent point. Standard checkpoints capture memory state, which can cause issues for apps like AD or databases on revert.","Manage virtual machines",[null,"Standard checkpoints capture memory and device state without guest VSS, so they are not application-consistent and can cause problems for AD or databases on revert.","Exporting a running VM with saved state creates a copy but is a slow full export, not a VSS-based application-consistent restore point.","Pausing a VM just suspends execution; it creates no restore point at all."]],["ws34",0,3,"VMs must talk to each other and to the Hyper-V host, but must never reach the physical network. Which virtual switch type do you create?",["External","Internal","Private","External with the management OS unchecked"],1,"An internal switch connects VMs and the host only. A private switch excludes the host; an external switch reaches the physical network.","Manage virtual machines",["An external switch binds to a physical NIC, so VMs would reach the physical network.",null,"A private switch connects VMs only to each other and excludes the Hyper-V host.","An external switch without management OS sharing still gives VMs access to the physical network, and it removes the host from the switch."]],["ws35",0,3,"You need to reconfigure a VM whose network adapter is disconnected, from the Hyper-V host, using PowerShell and guest credentials. What works?",["PowerShell Direct with Invoke-Command -VMName","Enter-PSSession -ComputerName","Remote Desktop to the VM","Windows Admin Center over HTTPS to the VM's management IP address"],0,"PowerShell Direct talks over the VMBus from host to guest with no network. -ComputerName uses WinRM over the network, which is down.","Manage virtual machines",[null,"Enter-PSSession -ComputerName uses WinRM over the network, which cannot work while the VM's adapter is disconnected.","Remote Desktop needs working network connectivity to the VM, which is exactly what is missing.","Windows Admin Center reaches servers over the network, so it cannot connect to a VM with no working network adapter."]],["ws36",0,3,"You have Windows Server Datacenter licenses with Software Assurance and want to lower the cost of Windows Server VMs in Azure. What should you enable?",["Azure Hybrid Benefit on the VMs","Reserved instances only","Spot pricing on production VMs","Automanage"],0,"Azure Hybrid Benefit applies existing licenses so you pay the base compute rate. Spot VMs can be evicted and are not a licensing benefit.","Manage virtual machines",[null,"Reserved instances discount compute for a one- or three-year commitment but do not use your existing Windows Server licenses; they can be combined with Hybrid Benefit but do not replace it.","Spot VMs can be evicted at any time, making them unsuitable for production, and they are a pricing tier, not a licensing benefit.","Automanage automates best-practice configuration of VMs; it does not reduce Windows licensing costs."]],["ws37",0,3,"You built a Windows Server VM in Azure as a template for many new VMs, each with a unique identity. What must you do before capturing it to an Azure Compute Gallery?",["Take a disk snapshot only","Enable boot diagnostics","Remove the VM agent","Run Sysprep /generalize /oobe /shutdown"],3,"Generalizing with Sysprep removes machine-specific data so each new VM gets its own SID and name. A snapshot of a specialized disk copies the identity.","Manage virtual machines",["A snapshot of a non-generalized disk copies the machine's identity, so every new VM would share the same SID and name settings.","Boot diagnostics captures console output for troubleshooting; it does not prepare an image for reuse.","The VM agent is needed for extensions and provisioning of new VMs; removing it breaks deployment rather than preparing the image.",null]],["ws38",0,3,"A branch Hyper-V host must copy a VM to a head-office host every 5 minutes so it can be started there if the branch fails, without shared storage or a cluster. What should you enable?",["Live migration","Shared-nothing storage migration","Hyper-V Replica","A production checkpoint schedule"],2,"Hyper-V Replica asynchronously replicates VM changes (30 seconds, 5 or 15 minutes) to another host for failover. Live migration moves a running VM once; it is not ongoing replication.","Manage virtual machines",["Live migration moves a running VM once between hosts; it does not provide ongoing replication for failover.","Shared-nothing storage migration also moves a VM once and requires the source to be online; it is not continuous replication.",null,"Checkpoints are stored alongside the VM on the same host, so they give no copy at head office if the branch fails."]],["ws39",0,3,"You want to prove that a Hyper-V Replica VM will boot at the DR site without interrupting replication or production. What should you run?",["Planned failover","Unplanned failover","Reverse replication","Test failover"],3,"Test failover creates a temporary copy of the replica VM, usually on an isolated network, while replication continues. Planned and unplanned failovers actually switch production.","Manage virtual machines",["A planned failover shuts down the primary and switches production to the replica, which interrupts the running VM.","An unplanned failover brings up the replica as production, for real disasters, and breaks the normal replication flow.","Reverse replication sends changes back from the replica after a failover; it does not test anything.",null]],["ws40",0,3,"A three-tier app on Hyper-V must fail over to Azure with the database first, then the app tier, then web servers, with one click. What should you configure in Azure Site Recovery?",["An Azure Backup policy for each VM","A replication policy with a shorter RPO","Three separate Recovery Services vaults","A recovery plan with ordered groups"],3,"Recovery plans group machines and control failover order with optional scripts between groups. A replication policy sets RPO and retention, not order.","Manage virtual machines",["Azure Backup protects VM data on a schedule; it provides no ordered, one-click failover to Azure.","A replication policy sets RPO, recovery point retention and snapshot frequency; it does not control failover order.","Splitting the tiers into separate vaults makes coordination harder and still provides no ordering of failover.",null]],["ws41",0,4,"Your DNS servers must resolve partner.local by sending those queries only to the partner's DNS servers, and send everything else to the ISP. What should you configure?",["A secondary zone for partner.local","A conditional forwarder for partner.local and a forwarder to the ISP","Root hints only","A DNS query resolution policy with separate zone scopes for partner.local and all other names"],1,"A conditional forwarder handles one domain and the forwarder handles the rest. A secondary zone needs zone transfers that the partner may not allow.","Implement and manage an on-premises and hybrid networking infrastructure",["A secondary zone requires zone transfers from the partner's server, which the partner may not allow, and it does not configure forwarding for everything else.",null,"Root hints make the server resolve names iteratively from the root, which cannot resolve a private partner.local namespace.","DNS policies can be used for query resolution rules, but that is far more complex than needed; a conditional forwarder plus a forwarder is the standard solution."]],["ws42",0,4,"The partner often changes its DNS server IPs and you keep updating conditional forwarders by hand. What keeps the name server list current automatically?",["A stub zone for the partner's domain","A reverse lookup zone","A GlobalNames zone","Disabling recursion"],0,"A stub zone holds the SOA, NS and glue records and refreshes them from the partner's servers. A conditional forwarder has static IPs.","Implement and manage an on-premises and hybrid networking infrastructure",[null,"A reverse lookup zone maps IP addresses to names; it does nothing to track the partner's name servers.","A GlobalNames zone provides single-label name resolution across a forest; it does not follow another domain's name servers.","Disabling recursion would stop the server from resolving the partner's names on behalf of clients at all."]],["ws43",0,4,"Internal clients must get the private IP for app.contoso.com while internet clients get the public IP, from the same DNS server. What should you implement?",["Two separate forwarders","Round robin","DNS policies with zone scopes (split-brain)","A CNAME to a GlobalNames zone"],2,"DNS policies with zone scopes answer differently by client subnet or interface. Round robin just rotates records among all clients.","Implement and manage an on-premises and hybrid networking infrastructure",["Forwarders decide where the server sends queries it cannot answer; they cannot return different answers for the same record based on who is asking.","Round robin rotates the order of multiple A records for all clients; it cannot give internal and external clients different addresses.",null,"GlobalNames zones hold single-label names and CNAMEs point to other names; neither can answer differently by client location."]],["ws44",0,4,"You want only domain members to register records in an AD-integrated zone. Which dynamic update setting should you use?",["Nonsecure and secure","None","Nonsecure only","Secure only"],3,"Secure only requires Kerberos authentication for updates, so only domain members can register. Nonsecure and secure lets anyone register or overwrite records.","Implement and manage an on-premises and hybrid networking infrastructure",["Nonsecure and secure accepts unauthenticated updates too, so any device could register or overwrite records.","None disables dynamic updates entirely, so domain members could not register their records either.","Nonsecure only is not an option in AD-integrated zones, and unauthenticated updates would let any device register.",null]],["ws45",0,4,"Azure VMs must resolve names in on-premises AD DNS, and on-premises servers must resolve an Azure private DNS zone, without running DNS VMs in Azure. What should you deploy?",["Azure DNS public zones","Azure DNS Private Resolver with inbound and outbound endpoints","Azure Front Door","A hosts file on each server, pushed and kept updated by Group Policy Preferences"],1,"The outbound endpoint and forwarding ruleset send on-premises domain queries back, and the inbound endpoint receives queries from on-premises forwarders. Public zones are for internet names.","Implement and manage an on-premises and hybrid networking infrastructure",["Azure DNS public zones host internet-facing names; they do not forward queries between Azure and on-premises private namespaces.",null,"Azure Front Door is a global HTTP load balancer and CDN; it does not resolve DNS for hybrid networks.","Hosts files do not scale, go stale when records change, and cannot resolve the Azure private DNS zone dynamically."]],["ws46",0,4,"Clients on VLAN 20 receive no DHCP addresses. The DHCP server is on VLAN 10 and scopes for both VLANs exist. What is the most likely fix?",["Create a superscope","Enable conflict detection","Deauthorize and reauthorize the DHCP server in Active Directory","Configure a DHCP relay (IP helper) on the VLAN 20 gateway"],3,"Routers do not forward DHCP broadcasts; a relay forwards them to the server and tells it which scope to use. A superscope is for several subnets on one physical segment.","Implement and manage an on-premises and hybrid networking infrastructure",["A superscope groups several subnets on the same physical segment; it does not carry DHCP broadcasts across a router to another VLAN.","Conflict detection pings addresses before leasing them; it does not help broadcasts from VLAN 20 reach the server.","Reauthorizing the server would not help because it already works for VLAN 10; the broadcasts from VLAN 20 simply never reach it.",null]],["ws47",0,4,"Each branch has a DHCP server, and a central server should take over only if a branch server fails, holding a small address reserve. Which failover mode fits?",["Hot standby","Load balance 50/50","Split scope 80/20 without failover","Superscope"],0,"Hot standby keeps one server active with the partner holding a reserve, ideal for a central backup. Load balance keeps both servers answering all the time.","Implement and manage an on-premises and hybrid networking infrastructure",[null,"Load balance mode keeps both servers answering requests all the time, which does not fit a central server that should only take over when the branch fails.","A split scope without failover divides addresses between servers statically and does not provide a coordinated takeover with a small reserve.","A superscope groups several subnets on one physical network; it is not a failover mode."]],["ws48",0,4,"A newly installed DHCP server in the domain is not handing out leases although its scope is active. What is most likely missing?",["A reverse lookup zone","Authorization of the server in Active Directory","A DHCP option 015","A static IP on the clients"],1,"Domain-joined DHCP servers must be authorized in AD before they lease addresses. Option 015 sets the DNS suffix but does not stop leasing.","Implement and manage an on-premises and hybrid networking infrastructure",["A reverse lookup zone is for DNS PTR records; its absence does not stop a DHCP server from leasing addresses.",null,"Option 015 sets the DNS domain name for clients; a missing option does not prevent leases.","Clients must use DHCP to get leases, so giving them static IPs is the opposite of what is needed."]],["ws49",0,4,"An admin set a static IP inside Windows on an Azure VM and the VM became unreachable. What is the correct approach?",["Keep the static IP inside Windows and add a matching DNS A record and an NSG rule","Use a public IP only","Make the IP static on the Azure NIC and leave Windows on DHCP","Add a second NIC with the same IP"],2,"Azure's DHCP hands the VM the address set on its NIC; making it static there keeps it fixed. A different address set in the guest breaks connectivity.","Implement and manage an on-premises and hybrid networking infrastructure",["Azure networking only delivers traffic for the IP assigned on the NIC, so a static address set inside Windows breaks connectivity; DNS records and NSG rules cannot fix that.","A public IP adds internet reachability but does not fix the guest's wrong network configuration, and the VM still needs a correct private IP.",null,"Two NICs cannot share the same IP in a VNet, and adding a NIC does not correct the guest's static settings."]],["ws50",0,4,"Head office needs its whole on-premises network connected to an Azure VNet over the internet with IPsec. Which components do you create in Azure?",["A NAT gateway and a public load balancer","A virtual network gateway, a local network gateway and a connection","An application gateway and a WAF policy","A DNS Private Resolver with an outbound endpoint and a private DNS zone linked to the VNet"],1,"A site-to-site VPN needs the VPN gateway, a local network gateway describing the on-premises device and address space, and a connection with a shared key. NAT gateways only handle outbound internet.","Implement and manage an on-premises and hybrid networking infrastructure",["A NAT gateway provides outbound internet for a subnet and a public load balancer spreads inbound traffic; neither builds an IPsec tunnel to on-premises.",null,"An application gateway with WAF is a web load balancer for HTTP traffic, not a site-to-site VPN.","DNS Private Resolver handles name resolution between networks; it does not create any network connectivity."]],["ws51",0,4,"A trading firm needs a private connection to Azure that does not travel over the public internet and offers predictable latency. What should it use?",["ExpressRoute","Point-to-site VPN","Site-to-site VPN over IKEv2","Azure Bastion"],0,"ExpressRoute uses a private circuit through a connectivity provider. Site-to-site VPN is encrypted but still crosses the internet.","Implement and manage an on-premises and hybrid networking infrastructure",[null,"Point-to-site VPN connects individual clients over the internet, so it is neither private nor predictable in latency.","Site-to-site VPN is encrypted but still travels over the public internet with variable latency.","Azure Bastion gives browser-based RDP/SSH to VMs; it is not a network connection between the firm and Azure."]],["ws52",0,5,"A branch file server has 2 TB of data but only a 1 TB disk. You want all files visible locally while rarely used files live in Azure. What should you configure?",["DFS Replication to an Azure VM","Azure File Sync with cloud tiering on the server endpoint","Storage Replica to Azure","BranchCache hosted cache"],1,"Cloud tiering keeps hot files local and replaces cold ones with pointers recalled on access. DFS-R copies full data and needs the same space on each end.","Manage storage and file services",["DFS Replication copies full files to each member, so the branch server would need space for all 2 TB.",null,"Storage Replica replicates whole volumes block by block between servers; it does not tier cold files to Azure while keeping them visible locally.","BranchCache caches content fetched over the WAN from a central server; it does not move cold data to Azure to free local disk space."]],["ws53",0,5,"A server endpoint has a volume free space policy of 20% and a date policy of 60 days. The volume is 85% full. What does tiering do?",["Tiers only files older than 60 days, even if that leaves the volume 85% full","Ignores both policies until the volume is full","Deletes files older than 60 days","Tiers files older than 60 days, then colder files until 20% is free"],3,"The free space policy always takes priority, so tiering continues past the date policy until 20% is free. Tiering never deletes files; it leaves stubs.","Manage storage and file services",["The volume free space policy takes priority over the date policy, so tiering will not stop while the volume is short of 20% free.","Both policies are applied continuously; tiering does not wait until the volume is completely full.","Cloud tiering never deletes files; it replaces cold files with stubs whose content stays in the Azure file share.",null]],["ws54",0,5,"How many cloud endpoints can a single Azure File Sync sync group contain?",["One","Two","Up to 30","One per server endpoint"],0,"A sync group has exactly one cloud endpoint (one Azure file share) and one or more server endpoints. Several server endpoints share that one cloud endpoint.","Manage storage and file services",[null,"A sync group has exactly one cloud endpoint; only server endpoints can be multiple.","There is no 30-cloud-endpoint option; a sync group always maps to exactly one Azure file share, however many servers join it.","The cloud endpoint count does not scale with server endpoints; many server endpoints share the single cloud endpoint."]],["ws55",0,5,"On-premises users must access an Azure file share with their AD DS credentials and NTFS permissions. What do you configure?",["A separate shared access signature token issued to each user with read and write rights","Anonymous access with a firewall rule","Storage account keys in a mapped drive","AD DS authentication for the storage account plus share-level RBAC roles"],3,"Joining the storage account to AD DS enables Kerberos, share-level Azure RBAC grants share access and NTFS ACLs apply to files. Storage account keys give full access and no per-user permissions.","Manage storage and file services",["SAS tokens are time-limited shared keys, not AD identities, and they bypass NTFS permissions for per-user access.","Azure Files does not allow anonymous SMB access, and firewall rules do not provide identity or NTFS permissions.","Storage account keys grant full admin access to the share for anyone using them, with no per-user identity or NTFS enforcement.",null]],["ws56",0,5,"Remote users need secure access to a file server over the internet without a VPN, using SMB over UDP 443. What should you enable?",["SMB over QUIC","SMB 1.0","SMB signing only","WebDAV redirector"],0,"SMB over QUIC tunnels SMB through TLS 1.3 on UDP 443. SMB signing protects integrity but does not change the transport or remove the need for port 445.","Manage storage and file services",[null,"SMB 1.0 is insecure and deprecated, and it still uses TCP 445 rather than UDP 443.","SMB signing protects integrity of sessions but still needs port 445 and does not provide a VPN-less internet transport.","WebDAV uses HTTP/HTTPS, not SMB over UDP 443, and it is not the secure SMB transport Windows Server provides for this."]],["ws57",0,5,"Users must be blocked from saving more than 2 GB to their home folders, and should get a warning email at 90%. What should you configure in FSRM?",["A soft quota with a notification","A hard quota with a 90% notification threshold","A passive file screen","A classification rule"],1,"A hard quota blocks writes at the limit, and thresholds send warnings. A soft quota only notifies and never blocks.","Manage storage and file services",["A soft quota only sends notifications and never blocks writes, so users could exceed 2 GB.",null,"A passive file screen only monitors blocked file types; it does not limit the amount of data saved.","Classification rules tag files with properties; they do not enforce storage limits."]],["ws58",0,5,"Users should reach \\\\contoso.com\\Public regardless of which server holds the folder, and folders should replicate between two sites. What should you deploy?",["A domain-based DFS namespace with DFS Replication","A standalone namespace on one server, replicated with Storage Replica to the second site","BranchCache in distributed mode","Storage Replica"],0,"A domain-based namespace gives a domain path that survives server changes, and DFS-R keeps folder targets in sync. A standalone namespace depends on one server's name.","Manage storage and file services",[null,"A standalone namespace path includes one server's name, and Storage Replica replicates a whole volume to a passive destination, which does not give active copies in both sites.","BranchCache caches content for WAN clients; it does not provide a domain-wide path or replicate folders between sites.","Storage Replica replicates volumes at block level to a standby destination that clients cannot use during normal operation, and it gives no unified namespace path."]],["ws59",0,5,"You have five disks in a storage pool and want to survive two simultaneous disk failures for VM storage with good write performance. Which resiliency should you pick?",["Simple","Single parity","Two-way mirror","Three-way mirror"],3,"A three-way mirror keeps three copies and survives two failures with good write performance. A two-way mirror only survives one failure, and parity writes are slower.","Manage storage and file services",["Simple spaces stripe data with no redundancy, so a single disk failure loses data.","Single parity survives only one disk failure and has slower writes, which is poor for VM storage.","A two-way mirror keeps two copies and survives only one disk failure.",null]],["ws60",0,5,"A new Hyper-V volume should give fast VHDX creation and merging through block cloning and detect corruption with integrity streams. Which file system fits?",["NTFS","FAT32","ReFS","exFAT"],2,"ReFS block cloning speeds up VHDX operations and checkpoint merges, and integrity streams detect corruption. NTFS lacks both.","Manage storage and file services",["NTFS does not support block cloning or integrity streams, so VHDX creation and checkpoint merges are slower.","FAT32 has a 4 GB file size limit, no security and no integrity features, making it unusable for VHDX storage.",null,"exFAT is designed for removable media and lacks block cloning, integrity streams and NTFS-style permissions."]],["ws61",0,5,"A two-node failover cluster spans two sites and there is no third site for a witness. What keeps quorum if one site goes down?",["A disk witness on the shared SAN","No witness at all, relying on dynamic quorum and dynamic witness weighting","A cloud witness in an Azure storage account","A file share witness on one of the nodes"],2,"A cloud witness uses a blob in Azure as the tie-breaking vote from outside both sites. A file share witness on a node or a site-local disk fails with that site.","Manage storage and file services",["A disk witness on the shared SAN lives in one site, and a stretched two-site cluster usually has no single disk both sites can reach; losing that site loses the witness.","Without a witness a two-node cluster can survive only certain orderly failures; dynamic quorum cannot reliably keep quorum when a whole site fails unexpectedly.",null,"A file share witness hosted on one of the nodes goes down with that node's site, so it provides no independent vote."]],["ws62",0,5,"You want cluster nodes patched one at a time with roles moved away first, without manual work each month. What should you enable?",["Azure Update Manager on each node independently","Cluster-Aware Updating in self-updating mode","WSUS with automatic restart at 3 AM on all nodes","Hyper-V Replica"],1,"Cluster-Aware Updating drains, updates and restarts nodes one after another. Independent patching with automatic restarts can take several nodes down together.","Manage storage and file services",["Patching each node independently with Update Manager does not coordinate draining roles or restarting nodes one at a time across the cluster.",null,"WSUS with an automatic restart at the same time on all nodes can take several or all nodes down at once, causing an outage.","Hyper-V Replica replicates VMs to another host for DR; it does not orchestrate patching of cluster nodes."]],["ws63",0,5,"Hyper-V hosts should store VHDX files on an active-active clustered SMB share that all nodes serve at once. Which clustered role do you create?",["General use file server","DFS Namespace server","iSCSI Target Server","Scale-Out File Server"],3,"Scale-Out File Server provides active-active continuously available SMB shares on CSVs for application data. The general use file server is active-passive and meant for user shares.","Manage storage and file services",["The general use file server role is active-passive, with the share served by one node at a time, and is meant for user file shares.","A DFS Namespace server provides a unified path to folders; it does not serve clustered active-active SMB storage for VHDX files.","iSCSI Target Server presents block storage over iSCSI, not an SMB share that all nodes serve at once.",null]],["ws64",0,5,"You must replicate a data volume between two data centers 5 ms apart with zero data loss at block level. What should you configure?",["Storage Replica in asynchronous mode","DFS Replication with a large staging quota","Azure File Sync with cloud tiering","Storage Replica in synchronous mode"],3,"Synchronous Storage Replica acknowledges writes only after both sites have them, giving zero data loss on low-latency links. Asynchronous mode can lose recent writes, and DFS-R works at file level.","Manage storage and file services",["Asynchronous Storage Replica acknowledges writes before the remote site has them, so recent writes can be lost during a failure.","DFS Replication works at the file level and is asynchronous, so it cannot guarantee zero data loss.","Azure File Sync syncs files to Azure asynchronously with tiering; it is not block-level, zero-data-loss replication between data centers.",null]],["ws65",0,5,"You plan Storage Spaces Direct for a Hyper-V cluster. Which statement is correct?",["It needs a shared SAS JBOD enclosure cabled to every node in the cluster","It needs a minimum of two servers using their local drives","It only supports parity resiliency","It must run on Windows Server Standard edition"],1,"Storage Spaces Direct pools local drives from 2 to 16 servers and requires Datacenter edition. Shared SAS enclosures are for classic clustered Storage Spaces, not S2D.","Manage storage and file services",["Shared SAS JBOD enclosures are used by classic clustered Storage Spaces; S2D instead pools each node's local drives.",null,"S2D supports mirror, parity and mirror-accelerated parity resiliency, not only parity.","Storage Spaces Direct requires Datacenter edition; Standard edition does not support it."]],["ws66",0,6,"Which statement about the Protected Users group is correct?",["Members can still use NTLM and DES for compatibility","It is designed for computer and service accounts, which then cache credentials","Members cannot use NTLM, and their Kerberos TGTs are not renewable past four hours","It replaces the need for Domain Admins"],2,"Protected Users blocks NTLM, DES/RC4 in Kerberos pre-auth, delegation and credential caching, with a four-hour TGT lifetime. It is intended for user accounts, not service or computer accounts.","Secure Windows Server infrastructure",["Protected Users members are blocked from NTLM authentication and cannot use DES or RC4 in Kerberos pre-authentication.","The group is meant for user accounts; putting computer or service accounts in it causes authentication failures, and members' credentials are not cached.",null,"Protected Users adds protections to accounts; it does not replace admin groups like Domain Admins or grant any privileges."]],["ws67",0,6,"Security wants RDP to Azure VMs closed at all times except for short, approved windows from a specific IP. Which feature does this?",["Azure Firewall DNAT","Just-in-time VM access in Defender for Cloud","A permanent NSG allow rule","Serial Console"],1,"JIT opens the port on the NSG only on request, for a limited time and from a chosen source. A permanent rule leaves the port open.","Secure Windows Server infrastructure",["Azure Firewall DNAT publishes ports through the firewall permanently unless rules are changed; it does not provide on-request, time-limited access.",null,"A permanent NSG allow rule leaves RDP open at all times, which the requirement forbids.","Serial Console gives text-based emergency console access through the portal; it does not control RDP port exposure."]],["ws68",0,6,"Attackers on a member server used a tool to dump NTLM hashes from LSASS memory. Which feature keeps these secrets out of reach of the normal OS?",["BitLocker","Credential Guard","Windows Defender Firewall","SMB signing"],1,"Credential Guard stores NTLM hashes and Kerberos TGTs in a VBS-isolated process that LSASS cannot expose. BitLocker protects data at rest, not memory.","Secure Windows Server infrastructure",["BitLocker encrypts disks to protect data at rest; it does not protect secrets in LSASS memory on a running system.",null,"Windows Defender Firewall filters network traffic; it cannot stop a local tool from reading LSASS memory.","SMB signing protects the integrity of SMB sessions against relay and tampering; it does not protect credentials in memory."]],["ws69",0,6,"You want to allow only approved, signed applications on file servers, but first learn what would be blocked without breaking anything. What should you do?",["Enforce an AppLocker policy immediately","Remove every user from the local Administrators group on each file server","Deploy an App Control for Business policy in audit mode","Enable SmartScreen"],2,"Audit mode logs would-be blocks in the CodeIntegrity log so you can tune the policy before enforcing. Enforcing straight away risks blocking needed software.","Secure Windows Server infrastructure",["Enforcing AppLocker immediately blocks anything not allowed, which risks breaking needed software before you know what would be blocked.","Removing local admins reduces privilege but does not control which applications can run or show what a policy would block.",null,"SmartScreen checks downloaded files and URLs for reputation; it is not an application allow-listing policy for servers."]],["ws70",0,6,"You want a Microsoft-recommended security baseline applied to Windows Server 2025 member servers with drift control that reapplies settings if they are changed. What should you use?",["OSConfig security baseline","A logon script that edits the registry","Security Configuration Wizard","Server Manager best practices analyzer"],0,"OSConfig applies the Windows Server 2025 baseline and remediates drift. The Security Configuration Wizard is gone from current Windows Server, and a logon script does not detect drift.","Secure Windows Server infrastructure",[null,"A logon script that edits the registry only runs at sign-in, is not a Microsoft baseline and does not detect or report drift.","The Security Configuration Wizard was removed from modern Windows Server and was never drift control.","The Best Practices Analyzer scans roles for configuration issues and reports; it does not apply a baseline or remediate drift."]],["ws71",0,6,"Every server shares the same local Administrator password, which an attacker could reuse for lateral movement. What fixes this with the least effort?",["Rename the Administrator account on each server","Disable NTLM across the domain","Store the password in a GPO preference","Windows LAPS with passwords backed up to AD DS"],3,"Windows LAPS gives each machine a unique rotated password stored with access control. Group Policy Preferences passwords are readable by any domain user and were removed for that reason.","Secure Windows Server infrastructure",["Renaming Administrator does not change the shared password, so it can still be reused once the new name is found.","Disabling NTLM domain-wide is a large, risky change and the identical password could still be reused for local or Kerberos sign-in on every server.","Group Policy Preferences stored passwords with a published key, so any domain user can read them; that feature was removed for this reason.",null]],["ws72",0,6,"Which practice best protects domain controllers from credential theft by admin accounts?",["Let help desk staff sign in to DCs to reset passwords","Allow only Tier 0 admins, using privileged access workstations","Install a web browser on DCs for downloading updates","Run file, print and DHCP services on the DCs to save on server hardware"],1,"Tiered administration keeps Tier 0 credentials off lower-tier machines and limits who signs in to DCs. Extra roles, browsing and help desk logons expand the attack surface.","Secure Windows Server infrastructure",["Letting help desk staff sign in to DCs exposes Tier 0 systems to lower-tier credentials and increases the risk of credential theft.",null,"Browsing the web from a DC exposes it to malicious content and phishing, which is exactly what should be avoided.","Running extra services on DCs increases their attack surface and the number of people who need access to them."]],["ws73",0,6,"A server must accept inbound TCP 1433 only from the app server subnet when on the domain network. Where should you create the rule?",["An outbound rule in the Private profile","A connection security rule with no filter","An inbound Windows Defender Firewall rule for the Domain profile, scoped to that subnet","A rule in the Public profile"],2,"An inbound allow rule scoped by remote address and bound to the Domain profile limits the port to that subnet on the domain network. Outbound rules do not control inbound connections.","Secure Windows Server infrastructure",["An outbound rule controls traffic leaving the server, not incoming connections to TCP 1433, and the Private profile is not the domain network.","A connection security rule configures IPsec authentication between hosts; with no filter it does not restrict inbound access to one subnet.",null,"The Public profile applies on untrusted networks, not on the domain network, so the rule would not apply when needed."]],["ws74",0,6,"You want vulnerability findings, security recommendations and EDR for on-premises Arc-enabled and Azure servers from one portal. What should you enable?",["Microsoft Defender for Servers in Defender for Cloud","Azure DDoS Protection","Azure Firewall Premium","Microsoft Entra ID Protection"],0,"Defender for Servers (via Defender for Cloud) provides recommendations, vulnerability assessment and Defender for Endpoint integration for Azure and Arc machines. Entra ID Protection covers identities, not servers.","Secure Windows Server infrastructure",[null,"Azure DDoS Protection defends public IPs against volumetric attacks; it offers no vulnerability findings or EDR for servers.","Azure Firewall Premium inspects network traffic with IDPS and TLS inspection; it does not assess servers or provide EDR.","Entra ID Protection detects risky sign-ins and users; it does not assess or protect servers."]],["ws75",0,6,"File servers must refuse unsigned SMB sessions to block relay attacks. What should you configure?",["Disable SMB 3","Enable SMB 1 for compatibility","Require SMB signing on the server","Enable SMB over QUIC only for internal clients"],2,"Requiring SMB signing makes the server reject unsigned sessions, which stops NTLM relay to SMB. Enabling SMB 1 weakens security.","Secure Windows Server infrastructure",["Disabling SMB 3 removes encryption and modern security features, weakening protection rather than blocking relay attacks.","SMB 1 is insecure and deprecated, and enabling it makes relay and other attacks easier.",null,"SMB over QUIC secures SMB transport over UDP 443 for remote clients, but it does not make the server reject unsigned SMB sessions on the normal network."]],["ws76",0,7,"You want performance counters and event logs from hybrid servers sent to a Log Analytics workspace. What do you configure with the Azure Monitor agent?",["A diagnostic setting on the subscription","An action group","A Recovery Services vault","A data collection rule"],3,"Data collection rules tell the Azure Monitor agent what to collect and where to send it. Action groups only define who is notified by alerts.","Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments",["A subscription diagnostic setting sends the Azure Activity log, not guest OS counters or event logs from servers.","An action group defines who gets notified and how when an alert fires; it does not collect data.","A Recovery Services vault stores backups and Site Recovery data, not monitoring logs.",null]],["ws77",0,7,"An Azure VM's Windows Firewall change blocked RDP and the VM has no Bastion. What is the quickest way to fix it from the portal?",["Redeploy the VM to a new Azure host, which resets the guest firewall configuration","Resize the VM","Use Run Command to run a PowerShell script that fixes the firewall rule","Attach a new NIC"],2,"Run Command executes a script through the VM agent without network access to the guest. Redeploying moves the VM but keeps the same firewall rule.","Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments",["Redeploying moves the VM to a new host but keeps the guest OS unchanged, so the bad firewall rule remains.","Resizing changes the VM's size and restarts it, but the guest firewall configuration stays the same.",null,"Adding a NIC does not change the Windows Firewall rules that are blocking RDP."]],["ws78",0,7,"You need to record CPU, memory, disk and network counters every 15 seconds for a week on a server and review them later. What should you create?",["A user-defined data collector set in Performance Monitor","A Resource Monitor session left open and exported at the end of the week","A custom view in Event Viewer","A Task Manager dump file"],0,"Data collector sets log counters on a schedule to a file for later analysis. Resource Monitor only shows live data.","Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments",[null,"Resource Monitor shows live data only and has no built-in scheduled logging over a week.","An Event Viewer custom view filters existing event logs; it does not record performance counters.","A Task Manager dump file captures one process's memory at one moment, not counters over time."]],["ws79",0,7,"You want Security events from 40 member servers gathered on one Windows server without buying a SIEM. What should you configure?",["A Performance Monitor alert","A scheduled task on each server that copies EVTX files","Windows Event Forwarding with a subscription on a collector server","System Insights on every server"],2,"Windows Event Forwarding sends selected events to a collector through subscriptions. Copying EVTX files is manual and slow, and System Insights forecasts capacity.","Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments",["A Performance Monitor alert triggers on counter thresholds; it does not gather Security events from other servers.","Copying EVTX files with scheduled tasks is manual, delayed and hard to query, compared with built-in event forwarding.",null,"System Insights predicts capacity trends using local data; it does not collect security events centrally."]],["ws80",0,7,"You want Windows Server to forecast when a volume will run out of space based on local history, without sending data to the cloud. Which feature does this?",["VM insights","Storage Replica","Azure Advisor","System Insights"],3,"System Insights runs local machine-learning models to predict CPU, network, volume and total storage consumption. VM insights sends data to Azure Monitor.","Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments",["VM insights sends performance and dependency data to Azure Monitor in the cloud, which the requirement rules out.","Storage Replica replicates volumes for DR; it does not forecast storage consumption.","Azure Advisor gives cloud resource recommendations based on Azure telemetry, not local on-premises forecasts.",null]],["ws81",0,7,"A member server fails Kerberos authentication with clock skew errors. What should you check first?",["The DNS zone's scavenging settings","w32tm /query /status and the time source hierarchy","The server's DHCP lease","The site link cost"],1,"Kerberos fails if clocks differ by more than five minutes by default; w32tm shows the source and offset. Scavenging and site links do not affect time.","Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments",["DNS scavenging removes stale records; it does not affect system time or Kerberos clock skew.",null,"A DHCP lease provides IP settings; it does not control the server's clock or cause Kerberos time errors.","Site link cost influences replication topology; it has no effect on time synchronization."]],["ws82",0,7,"The AD Recycle Bin was never enabled and an OU was deleted. Which recovery restores it and makes sure other DCs do not delete it again?",["A non-authoritative restore in DSRM","Restarting the NTDS service","An authoritative restore of the OU with ntdsutil in DSRM","Running repadmin /syncall /AdeP to pull the OU back from replication partners"],2,"After restoring system state in DSRM, marking the OU authoritative increases version numbers so it replicates out. A non-authoritative restore would be overwritten by the deletion from other DCs.","Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments",["A non-authoritative restore brings the DC back to the backup state, but the deletion from other DCs replicates in and removes the OU again.","Restarting the NTDS service does not bring back deleted objects.",null,"Replication partners also processed the deletion, so syncing with them only replicates the deletion again."]],["ws83",0,7,"SYSVOL on every DC was damaged by a bad script, and one DC has a good copy. What should you perform with DFSR?",["An authoritative SYSVOL restore from the good DC","A non-authoritative restore on the good DC","Reinstalling the DFS Namespaces role on every DC and recreating the share","Deleting the SYSVOL share on all DCs"],0,"An authoritative DFSR SYSVOL restore (msDFSR-Options=1 on the good DC) makes that copy the source for all others. A non-authoritative restore on the good DC would pull the bad data.","Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments",[null,"A non-authoritative restore on the good DC would make it pull SYSVOL from its partners, replacing the good copy with the damaged data.","SYSVOL is replicated by DFS Replication, not provided by the DFS Namespaces role, so reinstalling that role does not repair it.","Deleting the SYSVOL share breaks Group Policy and logon scripts domain-wide and does not restore good content."]],["ws84",0,7,"An on-premises Arc-enabled server shows as Disconnected in the portal. Which command checks its connectivity to the required Azure endpoints?",["nltest /sc_verify","Test-ComputerSecureChannel","Get-WindowsUpdateLog","azcmagent check"],3,"azcmagent check tests the agent's network access to the Arc endpoints. Test-ComputerSecureChannel checks the domain trust, not Azure connectivity.","Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments",["nltest /sc_verify checks the secure channel between a computer and its domain; it does not test Azure Arc endpoints.","Test-ComputerSecureChannel checks the domain trust with a DC, not connectivity to Azure.","Get-WindowsUpdateLog builds a readable Windows Update log; it has nothing to do with the Arc agent's connectivity.",null]],["ws85",0,7,"An Azure VM will not boot after an update and RDP is unavailable. What shows you the console output and a screenshot of where it stopped?",["Azure Advisor","Boot diagnostics","Network Watcher IP flow verify","Azure Activity log"],1,"Boot diagnostics captures serial log and screenshots of the VM's console. IP flow verify only tests NSG rules.","Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments",["Azure Advisor gives best-practice recommendations; it does not show a VM's console output or boot screen.",null,"IP flow verify tests whether NSG rules allow a packet; it cannot show why the OS did not boot.","The Activity log records control-plane operations on Azure resources, not what happens inside the guest during boot."]],["ws86",0,7,"A server can ping a web server's IP but cannot reach its HTTPS site. Which command quickly tests the TCP port?",["ping -t","ipconfig /registerdns","Test-NetConnection -ComputerName web01 -Port 443","nslookup web01"],2,"Test-NetConnection with -Port attempts a TCP connection and reports success. ping uses ICMP and says nothing about port 443.","Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments",["ping uses ICMP and only proves the host responds; it says nothing about whether TCP 443 is reachable.","ipconfig /registerdns re-registers the local computer's DNS records; it does not test a remote port.",null,"nslookup tests name resolution, which already works since the IP is known, and does not test the HTTPS port."]],["ws87",0,7,"A small office wants files and system state from one server backed up offsite to Azure without deploying a backup server. What should they install?",["Windows Server Backup to a USB disk","Microsoft Azure Backup Server (MABS)","Azure Site Recovery Mobility service","The Microsoft Azure Recovery Services (MARS) agent"],3,"The MARS agent backs up files, folders and system state straight to a Recovery Services vault. MABS is a full backup server, and Site Recovery is replication, not backup.","Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments",["Windows Server Backup to a USB disk keeps backups locally unless someone moves the disk, so it does not back up offsite to Azure.","MABS is a full on-premises backup server, which the office does not want to deploy.","The Site Recovery Mobility service replicates machines for disaster recovery; it is not a file and system state backup.",null]],["ws88",0,7,"You need to recover a failed server onto new hardware, including the OS, applications and data, from a local backup. Which Windows Server Backup type is required?",["A file and folder backup","A bare-metal recovery backup","A system state backup only","A Volume Shadow Copy schedule"],1,"Bare-metal recovery backups include everything needed to rebuild the server on new hardware. System state alone holds registry, boot files and AD, not full volumes.","Monitor and troubleshoot Windows Server environments",["A file and folder backup only includes selected data, not the OS and applications needed to rebuild the server.",null,"System state includes the registry, boot files and roles like AD, but not full volumes with applications and data.","Shadow copies are snapshots stored on the same volume and cannot rebuild a server onto new hardware."]]]);
